d.** I deleted tests for behaviour that no longer exists: the two `reveal` unit tests in `test/index.ts`, the hydration test that required the class on the scaffold, and the `not.toHaveClass(/reduce-fouce/)` lines in the e2e file.\n\n**Not tested.** The import order has no test of its own. I couldn't find anything observable that breaks with the old order, so I treated it as config and recorded that in the notes.\n\n**Docs:**\n- **README:** it no longer mentions `util.ts`. It now describes the \"write only when it differs\" pattern for any setup that writes server-rendered content, and explains how cascade changes the order modules run in. `toc:hydration` was re-run.\n- **AGENTS.md:** two notes for future work. Outside CI, Playwright reuses whatever server is already on port 8899, so a sibling worktree's older build can answer. And a MutationObserver has to collect records in its callback, because `takeRecords()` only returns ones not yet delivered.\n\n**Results:** `test:e2e` passes 15/15. One run failed only the three new tests together; the next eight runs were clean. My best guess is a sibling worktree's server on 8899, but I didn't confirm it. `test:hydration` (112 + 17), `lint`, `typecheck` and `npm test` (410) all pass. I deleted the build folder afterwards, and nothing is left listening on ports 8899 or 8123.","ttft_ms":5896,"type":"result","duration_ms":486844,"uuid":"80db9c9a-828e-46bd-989a-f51362f27585","ttft_stream_ms":5176,"time_to_request_ms":272,"first_content_frame_ms":5177,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
